{
  "challenges": [
    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_fwp_111",
      "name": "core-web-vitals-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Read the field data",
      "description": "Three statements about what Rice Rocket learned when it compared its lab scores with data from real visits. Decide whether each one is true.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "outcomes": [
        "measure-vitals"
      ],
      "points": 10,
      "hints": [
        "Ask what Lighthouse does during a run: does anyone tap a button? Which metric needs a real interaction to exist?",
        "Sort the visits and count to the 75th one. The assessment never looks at the average.",
        "INP attribution splits one interaction into input delay, processing duration and presentation delay. Which part did the network play in?"
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The team measured the menu page before release:\n\n```text\nLighthouse 12 (desktop, MacBook Pro M3, no throttling)\nPerformance  98\n  Largest Contentful Paint  0.9 s\n  Total Blocking Time       30 ms\n  Cumulative Layout Shift   0.01\n```\n\nStatement: \"A Lighthouse score of 98 shows that the menu page passes INP for real users.\"",
          "explanation": "False. Lighthouse is a lab test: one page load on one machine, and nobody taps anything, so it cannot measure Interaction to Next Paint at all (Total Blocking Time is only a lab proxy). The Core Web Vitals assessment uses field data: the 75th percentile of real visits, from the Chrome UX Report or your own RUM beacons, on the phones your users really have. A fast laptop without throttling hides exactly the long tasks a budget Android phone feels.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "INP of the /orders page over 100 visits, from the RUM table:\n\n```text\nvisits   INP\n  70     90 ms\n  30    520 ms\naverage 219 ms\n```\n\nStatement: \"The assessment reads the 75th percentile, which is 520 ms here, so the page is rated poor even though 70% of visits were fast.\"",
          "explanation": "True. Sorted, the 75th of 100 visits is one of the 520 ms ones, and an INP above 500 ms is poor (good is at most 200 ms, poor above 500 ms). The 75th percentile is chosen on purpose: a page passes only when at least three visits in four are good. The average (219 ms) hides the 30% of users who wait half a second after every tap.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The attribution build of web-vitals sent this for a slow tap on \"Add to cart\":\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"INP\",\n  \"value\": 610,\n  \"attribution\": {\n    \"interactionTarget\": \"button.add-to-cart\",\n    \"inputDelay\": 12,\n    \"processingDuration\": 540,\n    \"presentationDelay\": 58\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStatement: \"Most of the time is spent in the event handlers (processing duration), so a faster network or CDN will not fix this INP; the handler work has to shrink or yield.\"",
          "explanation": "True. INP measures from the tap to the next frame on screen: input delay (waiting for the main thread), processing duration (the event handlers) and presentation delay (rendering the frame). Here 540 of 610 ms are the handlers themselves, work done on the main thread after the tap. No request is part of that number; the fix is less work in the handler, or yielding so the frame can paint first (next lesson).",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "publicId": "chal_coding_fwp_112",
      "name": "rum-vitals-summary",
      "type": "mini_coding",
      "title": "Summarize the Core Web Vitals beacons",
      "description": "Rice Rocket's RUM endpoint collects the beacons that the web-vitals library sends from real visits (`onLCP`, `onINP`, `onCLS`, attribution build). Complete `summarize(beacons)`, which turns them into one line per page and metric the way the Core Web Vitals assessment reads them. The editor holds the module with its key blocks missing, each marked by a `// [ ]` line; some pieces are wrong on purpose. Rules: web-vitals reports INP and CLS again when they grow, so for each `id` only the last beacon counts; `p75` is the 75th percentile by nearest rank (sort ascending, take item `ceil(0.75 * n)`); the rating uses the published thresholds (LCP 2500 / 4000 ms, INP 200 / 500 ms, CLS 0.1 / 0.25: at or below the first is good, at or below the second needs improvement, above is poor); `culprit` is the attribution `target` that appears most often among the samples at or above p75 (ties: alphabetical). Run feeds it batches of beacons.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "outcomes": [
        "measure-vitals"
      ],
      "points": 30,
      "hints": [
        "Work one page and metric at a time: which beacons are really the same visit, what is the 75th value after sorting, which band does it fall in, and who owns the slow samples?",
        "Look at a Map keyed by `id` (a later `set` replaces an earlier one), `Math.ceil(values.length * 0.75) - 1` as an index into the sorted values, and comparisons with `<=` against each threshold pair.",
        "A p90 of API latencies by route with a slowest-endpoint owner looks like: group by route, `const sorted = ms.sort((a, b) => a - b); const p90 = sorted[Math.ceil(sorted.length * 0.9) - 1];`, then count `owner` over the calls with `ms >= p90` and take the most frequent."
      ],
